  "text": "She poured a year's wages on the floor. The man who complained about the waste was skimming the money. John twelve. Six days before Passover, in Bethany. Supper. Martha is serving. Lazarus is at the table, which is its own sentence. And Mary takes a pound of ointment of spikenard, very costly, and anoints the feet of Jesus, and wipes his feet with her hair. And the house was filled with the odour of the ointment. Everybody in that room knows exactly what just happened. Because they can smell it. Then Judas speaks up. And he uses the language of compassion. \"Why was not this ointment sold for three hundred pence, and given to the poor?\" Three hundred pence. Going by a penny being a day's wage elsewhere in the Gospels, that is roughly a year of work. Poured out on the floor. So on the face of it, that is not a stupid question. Except John tells us the motive outright. \"This he said, not that he cared for the poor; but because he was a thief, and had the bag.\" He was skimming. The man doing the maths on her devotion was stealing from the fund. And Jesus does not let her sit there and take it. \"Let her alone: against the day of my burying hath she kept this.\" He defended her out loud. In the room. In front of the man counting.",
